# 3. ADD A REAL INTERACTIVE TUTORIAL

A cinematic introduction alone is not enough.

After the introduction, the child must complete a guided tutorial before entering the main mission.

The tutorial should teach controls through actions rather than long blocks of text.

Use a friendly guide character or the rover itself.

The guide must explain one action at a time.

## Tutorial sequence

### Tutorial 1 — Look around

Display:

"Welcome, Explorer! Let's look around."

Highlight the camera controls.

Ask the player to:
- Rotate the camera.
- Zoom in.
- Zoom out.
- Switch to another camera view.

After the player performs the action, confirm:

"Great! You can now explore the Moon from different views."

### Tutorial 2 — Meet your rover

Explain:

"This is your rover. It can travel across the lunar surface, scan rocks and collect samples."

Ask the player to move it a short distance.

Show the movement controls on screen.

Do not advance until the player has successfully performed the action, but provide a skip option.

### Tutorial 3 — Learn the mission map

Explain:

"This map shows where your rover is and where you can explore."

Highlight:
- Rover position.
- Destination markers.
- Dangerous terrain.
- Explored areas.
- Available mission objectives.

### Tutorial 4 — Draw a rover route

This is essential and must be fully functional.

Explain:

"Instead of driving every second, you can draw a path for your rover to follow!"

Show a short demonstration of drawing a path and starting rover movement.

Guide the child through drawing a small route and pressing START.

### Tutorial 5 — Scan an object

Highlight a nearby rock.

Explain:

"Scientists use special instruments to learn what rocks are made of. Let's scan this one!"

Ask the player to move close to the rock and activate the scanner.

Display a simple result and explain what the scanner discovered.

### Tutorial completion

Display:

"You're ready, Explorer!"

Show a short mission checklist:

- You learned how to look around.
- You learned how to move your rover.
- You learned how to draw a route.
- You learned how to scan objects.

Give the child a small first achievement.

Do not make the tutorial a separate slideshow. It must teach the actual game controls and systems.

