# 14. TECHNICAL IMPLEMENTATION REQUIREMENTS

Work with the existing stack wherever possible.

If the current project uses React, retain React. If it uses Three.js or React Three Fiber, build upon the existing scene and rendering architecture.

Use appropriate systems for:
- Pointer-based route drawing.
- Terrain raycasting.
- Route projection.
- Collision detection.
- Terrain-aware movement.
- Path validation.
- Rover navigation.
- Animation state management.
- Mission state persistence.
- Task progression.
- Contextual guidance.
- Save/resume.

Keep game logic separate from UI presentation.

Use a single reliable mission state so that:
- The task panel.
- The mission map.
- Rover behaviour.
- Resource inventory.
- Animation states.
- Task completion.
- Butterfly effects.

All respond to the same actual game state.

Avoid hardcoded UI that claims a task is completed without checking the underlying gameplay state.

# 15. SCIENTIFIC ACCURACY

The game is educational, so visual realism must not come at the cost of scientific accuracy.

Follow these rules:

- Do not depict ordinary atmospheric wind or Earth-like sandstorms on the Moon.
- Do not show a blue atmospheric lunar sky.
- Do not treat hydrogen detection as confirmed accessible water ice.
- Do not apply one fixed lunar temperature or darkness duration to every South Pole location.
- Do not imply that a solar particle event directly measures radiation dose at the outpost.
- Distinguish archived NASA observations from live data and simulated gameplay.
- Explain that rover dust effects are local surface disturbance, not wind-driven storms.
- Distinguish real terrain data from procedural or simulated terrain.
- Do not invent NASA measurements.

Whenever NASA data is used, identify its source, observation date, coverage and whether it is observed, derived or simulated.

