Do not attempt to implement all upgrades in one untested pass.

Follow this sequence.

**Stage 1 — Existing project audit**
- Inspect code and existing gameplay.
- Preserve good camera controls.
- Identify broken and missing features.

**Stage 2 — Introduction and tutorial**
- Build the cinematic introduction.
- Add rover naming.
- Implement the interactive tutorial.
- Test every tutorial action.

**Stage 3 — Mission Guidance System**
- Add task popups.
- Add persistent objectives.
- Add contextual hints.
- Add task-completion explanations.
- Verify every phase has guidance.

**Stage 4 — Rover path drawing**
- Implement pointer-based route drawing.
- Project routes onto terrain.
- Validate obstacles and slopes.
- Implement actual path-following.
- Test route correction and pause/resume.

**Stage 5 — Visual and animation upgrade**
- Improve terrain and lighting.
- Improve rover animations.
- Add dust, impact and camera effects.
- Make visual effects respond to game state.

**Stage 6 — Four-phase integration**
- Connect exploration, resource collection, construction and survival.
- Verify persistent resources and butterfly effects.
- Verify every new task introduces itself.

**Stage 7 — Final quality assurance**
- Test the entire game from a fresh start.
- Test saved-game resume.
- Test failed and corrected routes.
- Test mouse and touch.
- Test audio off and reduced motion.
- Test missing NASA data and network failure.
- Test that every button works.

Do not proceed to the next stage while critical functionality in the current stage is broken.

